---
import Film from '../../plate/Film.astro';
import { renderFormula } from '../../../lib/formulas/render';
import { CUES, TIMELINE } from './story';
import './style.css';

const labels = [
  { id: 'ac-node', tex: String.raw`\mathrm{AC}` },
  { id: 'pp-node', tex: String.raw`\mathrm{PP}` },
  { id: 'acwo-node', tex: String.raw`\mathrm{AC}_{\mathrm{WO}}` },
  { id: 'con-zf', tex: String.raw`\operatorname{Con}(\mathrm{ZF})\Longrightarrow` },
  { id: 'con-extension', tex: String.raw`\operatorname{Con}(\mathrm{ZF}+\mathrm{PP}+\mathrm{AC}_{\mathrm{WO}}+\neg\mathrm{AC})` },
] as const;
---

<Film
  tag="uruk-choice-pieces"
  cues={CUES}
  timeline={TIMELINE}
  step={50}
  labels={labels}
  description="Six points are divided into pieces of sizes one, two and three. The same three arrows first select points inside their own pieces, then move to an injective assignment outside them. The arrows carry into their actual position on a board of all one hundred and twenty finite injections. Six board cells mark assignments that select inside every piece. The board folds into a history line showing Levi's 1902 question, three withdrawn preprints and the new paper. The original pieces shrink into the Axiom of Choice node of a known implication diagram. Its historical dashed return question is cut in blue beside a conditional relative-consistency claim. The closing ledger distinguishes the paper's unreviewed status, its dated expert reading, the catalogue statement with an inaccessible-cardinal assumption and a separately linked relative-consistency statement."
  caption="One finite set carries the difference between choosing inside a piece and merely assigning distinct points. The finite enumeration gives way to the historical question and the paper's blue relative-consistency claim. The finite picture supplies no infinite set-theory model."
>
  <p class="film-method">How the film is made. The browser enumerates all injections from three pieces into six points. The pieces contain point zero, points one and two, and points three through five. There are one hundred and twenty injective assignments and six that pick from inside every piece. Of the other one hundred and fourteen assignments, thirty-eight pick outside all three pieces, fifty pick inside exactly one piece and twenty-six pick inside exactly two pieces. The selected outside assignment sends the three pieces to points four, zero and one. Every point and piece keeps its identity while the arrows move.</p>
  <p class="film-method">The board lists the assignments in lexicographic order across twelve columns and ten rows. The outside example's three arrows first travel into its actual board cell. The remaining miniature triplets then complete distinct cells in order, so the running count counts each assignment once. Filled cells mark the six assignments that pick inside all three pieces. The board becomes a mark on the history line, and the original pieces later shrink into the Choice node. These are finite illustrations, not witnesses to an infinite countermodel or evidence that a consistency statement is true.</p>
  <p class="film-method">Over ZF, the Axiom of Choice implies the Partition Principle, which implies Choice for well-ordered families. The diagram keeps these known implications in ink. The dashed return from the Partition Principle to Choice is the historical question. The blue cut and tag state the paper's claim, <span set:html={renderFormula(String.raw`\operatorname{Con}(\mathrm{ZF})\Longrightarrow\operatorname{Con}(\mathrm{ZF}+\mathrm{PP}+\mathrm{AC}_{\mathrm{WO}}+\neg\mathrm{AC})`, false)} />. This is Theorem 1.1's syntactic relative-consistency statement. It assumes consistency of ZF rather than the existence of a transitive ground model. Its claim uses neither atoms nor large cardinals. The finite arrows do not establish any of these infinite-set assertions.</p>
  <p class="film-method">The historical line preserves the article's dates: Beppo Levi's statement in 1902, three withdrawn preprints in 2025 and 2026, and the forty-four-page paper posted on 24 September 2026. Distances on the line and the page icons are schematic. The article's evidence date remains 18 October 2026. Karagila read part of the paper, rejected its exposition, reported no mathematical error and said he would not read further. That record supplies neither mathematical approval nor a finding of an error. The film keeps the article's unreviewed status.</p>
  <p class="film-method">The release's scope note links two different Lean targets. The catalogued <code>PartitionPrinciple.lean</code> has an internal inaccessible-cardinal assumption, stronger than Theorem 1.1. The linked <code>PartitionConsistency.lean</code> matches Theorem 1.1's relative-consistency statement but is not catalogued. The linked targets contain placeholder declarations; their presence does not establish a checked proof of the paper. This film performs no proof replay, and no outside replay was found in the dated evidence. A kernel check concerns a formal statement and does not count as reading or approving the paper.</p>
  <p class="film-method">With JavaScript disabled, the article and transcript remain available without a fallback illustration. Reduced motion selects a completed supporting frame for each of the eleven cues. All subtitle text and source references follow the direction's canonical cue file, including its documented clause cut in the sentence about Levi.</p>
</Film>

<script>
  import './element';
</script>
